import Native

{-
   Text, as values - the integer half.

   The dual of `Show`, and a file of `Core` on the same terms as `Show.yana` beside it: it is named
   from anywhere in the module with no import and no `pub`, because a module is a directory.

   The class, the error and the integer instances are here; the float instances are
   `Float/Read.yana`, with the algorithm they need. The ASCII tier - the unit predicates, the
   scanning family and the case fold - is `Text.yana`, which reads nothing from this file and which
   this file reads nothing from: a digit's value in a radix is `readDigits`' own question and is not
   the same one `hexValue` answers.
-}

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `Read` - the dual of `Show`, Analysis-Library.md §2.1.
   ==========================================================================================

   Tritium spells parsing as sixteen template specializations of

       template<class T> T read(const char*& text, Size length);

   with failure signalled by *not advancing the cursor* - which a caller cannot observe unless it
   saved the pointer first, and which every one of the sixteen has to be trusted to get right. The
   dual of `Show` is a class with one member, and the failure is in the type:

       pub class Read(a):
         fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, a)

   **The cursor is a `&` parameter and the text is not.** A successful read advances it past what it
   consumed and a failed one leaves it exactly where it was, so a caller may try one instance and
   then another without saving anything - and a caller who wants the position back after a success
   still has it, because it is their own variable. The C++ convention is the same promise made where
   nobody can check it.

   **Text and cursor rather than a slice**, which is where this parts company with the analysis's
   sketch of `read(from: &Flat(CodeUnit))`. A `String` and an index into it costs what a slice would,
   since `String.unit` is a load on one target and a `charCodeAt` on the other.

   **The portability half of that argument is superseded, and the shape still stands.** It used to
   read "a slice of code units is a native-only thing: a JS string has no addressable units at all",
   which is the same reason `Chunked(String, CodeUnit)` above is `@platform(native)`.
   Analysis-Spellings.md item 13 settles that: `String` in a *binding* position becomes a shared
   view - `{items, length}` natively, and on JS the bare host string, where `hostSubstring` yields an
   O(1) `SlicedString` that is still a `string` to every host API. So a view is affordable on both
   targets, and when item 13 lands `from` becomes one for free: slicing a line out before reading it
   will cost nothing, with no signature here changed.

   **What keeps the cursor is the failure protocol, not the missing type.** A read has to say where
   it stopped, and item 13's position rule has no spelling for an *exclusive* view to advance in
   place - `String` binding is the shared view and `&String` binding is the owner, because a string
   has no writable element window. The alternatives are answering the remainder, which is
   `-> Result(ReadError, {a, String})` and stops `?` composing at every call site, or a `Cursor`
   record holding exactly this pair under a name. So the pair stays until there is a reason it
   cannot.

   **It composes with `?` immediately** - `let n = read(text, at)?` inside a function returning a
   `Result(ReadError, _)` - which makes this the first substantial user of the `Try` machinery in
   Core. Which instance is selected comes from the expected type, exactly as it does for `bitcast`.

   `readRadix` below is the sixteen `readHex`es collapsed into an argument.
-}

{-
   Why a read did not produce a value.

   Two reasons and not one, because a caller does different things about them: `NoDigits` is "this
   is not a number and you should try something else", which is what a parser reaching an unexpected
   token has, and `OutOfRange` is "this is a number and it is not one of these", which is a value
   error the input has to be blamed for. Neither carries a position - the caller's cursor is the
   position, and it did not move.
-}
pub data ReadError = NoDigits | OutOfRange

pub class Read(a):
  fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, a)

{-
   One digit's value in a radix, or `-1` for a unit that is not one.

   Letters count from ten in either case, which is what makes `readRadix(text, at, 16)` accept
   `ff` and `FF` and what makes every radix up to 36 spelled the same way. A unit at or above the
   radix is not a digit *here*, which is why the test is against the radix rather than against `9`.

   **Three range tests in sequence, and a shorter spelling was tried and lost.** `unit or 32` folds
   the two alphabets together and one test then covers both - it is fewer instructions and it is
   slower, because the arms below are if-converted into a select chain and an early `return` out of
   the middle of one is a branch that chain does not have. Measured at +6% on `test/bench/numbers`'s
   integer read; see §62.3.
-}
fn digitValue(unit: Int, radix: Int) -> Int:
    let value = match unit:
        "0"..="9" -> unit - "0"
        "a"..="z" -> unit - "a" + 10
        "A"..="Z" -> unit - "A" + 10
        _         -> -1

    if value >= radix then return -1
    return value

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Reading digits a vector at a time - Implementation-Vector.md §9 item 8's family, applied to the
   reading direction.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   **This is where a vector earns its keep in this library, and the direction is the reason.**
   Writing a number, the digits are produced one at a time out of a register and would have to be
   *inserted* into a lane one at a time, which costs more than the arithmetic it would replace - see
   `Core/Show.yana`, where that was measured and declined. Reading one, they arrive in memory sixteen
   at a time already, so one load answers both questions a digit loop asks: where the digits stop,
   and what eight of them are worth.

   Three functions rather than one, and the split is what keeps a short number from paying for a long
   one. `digitOffsets` is the load; `digitRunOf` is the compare, which every caller wants; and
   `eightDigitsOf` is the splitting tree, which only a caller with eight digits in hand wants and
   which is the expensive half. They take and answer a `Vec(U8)` so that a loop reading twenty-four
   digits loads three times rather than six.
-}

{-
   **Sixteen lanes, written down, and not the target's natural width** - which is the one thing here
   that is not obvious and is not negotiable.

   `interleaveLow` is a *portable* whole-vector operation: at sixteen lanes it is one instruction on
   every x86 back to SSE2. At thirty-two it was refused at `avx2` until J236 (every shuffle at 256 bits
   works inside each 128-bit half) and is two unpacks and a `vperm2f128` since; at `avx512` it is
   `vpermi2b` and an index vector (Analysis-Avx512.md §2.5). That refusal was the first reason for the
   pin, and J238, which moved the codec bodies to the natural width, left this one pinned for the
   reason that remains: sixteen is all *this* needs - the tree below reads eight digits and nothing here
   scales with the width, so a wider load would buy nothing and cost the wider interleave. The count is
   stated and the same code runs at every tier (test/bench/avx512/findings.md §5).

   `vectorPast` rather than `loadVectorTail` because the load has to be sixteen units wide whatever
   the natural width is. It takes a raw address and promises nothing, and what makes it
   safe is the same fact §8's guarantee rests on - a `String`'s units are a run the language allocated
   and padded, so sixteen bytes from anywhere inside it are readable. What is past the end is whatever
   the padding holds, so it may look like a digit, and `digitRunOf` is handed the count that says how
   many lanes were ever the string's.
-}

-- The units at `at`, biased so that a digit is `0` to `9` and nothing else is.
@platform(native) fn digitOffsets(text: String, at: Size) -> Vec(U8, 16):
    let raw = vectorPast(units(text) + at) :: Vec(CodeUnit, 16)
    return bitcast(raw - (splat("0" :: CodeUnit) :: Vec(CodeUnit, 16))) :: Vec(U8, 16)

-- How many leading lanes are digits, never more than the string has left. One compare and a
-- `firstSet`, which is the operation that makes a vector search terminate correctly - see Core's own
-- note on it, and note that its answer when nothing is set is the lane count, which is what makes a
-- window of nothing but digits fall out as "all of them".
@platform(native) fn digitRunOf(offset: Vec(U8, 16), available: Int) -> Int:
    let run = firstSet(offset .> (splat(9) :: Vec(U8, 16)))
    return if run < available then run else available

{-
   The first eight lanes as a number.

   The tree is the standard one and every step halves the lane count by doubling the width.
   `interleaveLow` against zero spreads eight byte lanes into sixteen-bit ones **without leaving the
   register**. That is also what `unpackLow` becomes on x64 (`expandLaneCountConversions`), but
   `unpackLow` takes a vector at the natural width and this reads sixteen lanes on every tier, so the
   interleave is written out. Read as thirty-two-bit lanes, each of the four then holds two adjacent
   digits - the earlier one in the low half, since the units sit in the register little-endian - so
   one masked multiply and add turns four lanes of two digits into four numbers below a hundred, and
   the four are weighted and summed as scalars.

   Measured 1.60x against the scalar loop over the same eight units; §62.4 of test/bench/findings.md.
-}
@platform(native) fn eightDigitsOf(offset: Vec(U8, 16)) -> U64:
    let spread = interleaveLow(offset, zero() :: Vec(U8, 16))
    let asPairs = bitcast(spread) :: Vec(U32, 4)
    let pairs = (asPairs `and` (splat(65535) :: Vec(U32, 4))) * (splat(10) :: Vec(U32, 4)) +
                (asPairs `shr` (splat(16) :: Vec(U32, 4)))

    return (widen(lane(pairs, 0)) :: U64) * 1000000 + (widen(lane(pairs, 1)) :: U64) * 10000 +
           (widen(lane(pairs, 2)) :: U64) * 100 + (widen(lane(pairs, 3)) :: U64)

{-
   Eight more digits at `at`, where there are eight and they all are.

   The shared spelling of the three above, for a caller whose loop is not `readMagnitude`'s -
   `Float/Read.yana`'s scan, which has a decimal point and an exponent to watch for and so cannot be a
   counted loop. It answers `False` rather than a count, because that caller only ever wants a whole
   block: a short tail is one it is going to read a unit at a time anyway.

   `False` on JS, where a string has no addressable units, which is what lets the scan stay one body
   instead of two.
-}
@platform(native) fn eightMoreDigits(text: String, at: Size, &value: U64) -> Bool:
    -- Eight units have to exist, which is what the caller wants anyway and is also what keeps the
    -- sixteen-unit window inside Design-Vector §5.1's guarantee - it starts at least eight units
    -- before the end, so it reaches at most eight bytes past it.
    if at + 8 > length(text) then return False

    let offset = digitOffsets(text, at)
    if digitRunOf(offset, 8) < 8 then return False

    value = eightDigitsOf(offset)
    return True

@platform(js) fn eightMoreDigits(text: String, at: Size, &value: U64) -> Bool = False

{-
   The digits at `at`, as an unsigned magnitude, with `limit` the largest one the caller will take.

   The cursor is advanced only on the way out, and only through the copy: a caller of this one is
   about to decide whether the magnitude fits its own type, and a read that is rejected there has to
   leave the cursor where an unread one would.

   **Overflow is detected before it happens rather than after**, because computing the product and
   comparing it would already have wrapped at a `limit` at the top of the type.

   **The division in it is not a division.** Every call site in this file supplies a literal radix
   and a literal limit, so the strength reduction turns `(limit - digit) / radix` into a multiply and
   a shift before it is ever executed. Hoisting it by hand - keeping `limit / radix` and its remainder
   and comparing against the pair - was written and reverted: it replaces one folded division with a
   two-compare chain that depends on the accumulator, and it is **42% slower** on
   `test/bench/numbers`'s integer read. §62.3.

   **The accumulator is `w` and not a `U64`, and that is the whole of the width story on the reading
   side.** `Magnitude(a -> w)` already picks the narrowest type a *magnitude* needs - `U32` up to
   thirty-two bits, the word's unsigned type for an index, `U64` for the three that need it - and
   `Core/Bits.yana` states what answering `U64` for all twelve cost the writing direction. It cost
   the reading direction the same thing and for longer: a `U64` is a `BigInt` on JavaScript, so every
   multiply, every compare and every division in this loop was a bignum operation to read a number
   that never left thirty-two bits. `read(text, at) :: Int` emitted nine `BigInt` calls under the
   `U64` accumulator and emits none under this one, and a program that never names a 64-bit type
   never reaches a `BigInt` at all.

   The constraints are the price of that dependency and they are the same four `Format/Number.yana`'s
   digit writer pays: `Integral(w)` is the arithmetic, `Ord(w)` the two comparisons, `TrivialCopy(w)`
   what lets the accumulator be a mutable local, and `Narrow(Int, w)` what brings a digit and the
   radix down from the `Int` they arrive at. What truncates there is a number below thirty-seven.
-}
fn (Integral(w), Ord(w), TrivialCopy(w), Narrow(Int, w))
  readDigitsAt(text: String, from: Size, radix: Int, limit: w, &stop: Size, &value: w) -> Maybe(ReadError):
    let units = length(text)
    let base = truncate(radix) :: w

    let &at = from
    let &seen = 0 :: w
    let &any = False
    let &over = False

    while at < units:
        let digit = digitValue(String.unit(text, at), radix)
        if digit < 0 then break

        let wide = truncate(digit) :: w
        if wide > limit || seen > (limit - wide) / base then over = True
        else seen = seen * base + wide

        any = True
        at = at + 1

    if !any then return Just(NoDigits)
    if over then return Just(OutOfRange)

    stop = at
    value = seen
    return Nothing

{-
   And the same magnitude with the units read a vector at a time, which is what a native build does
   for radix ten.

   **Nineteen digits is the whole design.** Nineteen nines is below `2^64`, so a run that short cannot
   wrap the accumulator - which means no per-digit overflow test, no per-digit range test either
   (the compare that produced the run already checked every lane), and one comparison against `limit`
   at the end instead of one per digit. Two blocks of eight and a tail of at most three is every
   decimal number a `U64` holds bar the twenty-digit ones, and those fall through to the loop above
   along with every other radix.

   The loop reloads rather than shifting the vector along, because a load that hits L1 is cheaper than
   the lane rearrangement would be and because the next window's *run* has to be measured anyway.

   Measured on `test/bench/numbers`: **3.45x** on a corpus of fifteen to nineteen digit values and
   **1.45x** on one of one to ten - §62.4, where the two arrangements that lose are also written down.
   The short corpus gains as much as it does because the run length is what turns the tail into a
   counted loop; an arrangement where the block was tried without measuring first was 12% *slower*
   there, since a four-digit number paid for a splitting tree it could not use.
-}
@platform(native) fn readMagnitude(text: String, from: Size, radix: Int, limit: U64, &stop: Size, &value: U64) -> Maybe(ReadError):
    let units = length(text)

    -- **A window is only ever loaded where there is a unit in it**, which is why this reads `from >=
    -- units` rather than letting the count sort it out. Design-Vector §5.1 guarantees the readable
    -- bytes past an object's *last* byte, so a whole vector starting one past the end asks for one
    -- byte more than the guarantee names - which the allocators happen to give and which is exactly
    -- the kind of thing to not be relying on. The same test is the reason the loop below reloads
    -- under a branch.
    if radix != 10 || from >= units then return readDigitsAt(text, from, radix, limit, stop, value)

    let &at = from
    let &seen = 0 :: U64
    let &count = 0 :: Int

    let &offset = digitOffsets(text, at)
    let &run = digitRunOf(offset, truncate(units - at) :: Int)

    -- At most two blocks: a third would put the count past nineteen and the no-wrap argument with it.
    while run >= 8 && count <= 8:
        seen = seen * 100000000 + eightDigitsOf(offset)
        at = at + 8
        count = count + 8
        run = 0

        if at < units:
            offset = digitOffsets(text, at)
            run = digitRunOf(offset, truncate(units - at) :: Int)

    if run >= 8 || count + run > 19 then return readDigitsAt(text, from, radix, limit, stop, value)
    if count + run == 0 then return Just(NoDigits)

    for i in 0 .. run:
        seen = seen * 10 + wideCount(String.unit(text, at + (i :: Size)) - "0")

    if seen > limit then return Just(OutOfRange)

    stop = at + (run :: Size)
    value = seen
    return Nothing

-- On JS a string has no addressable units to load, so eight of them are eight `charCodeAt` calls and
-- the loop above is already that. One name, split at the natural boundary rather than at every caller.
@platform(js) fn readMagnitude(text: String, from: Size, radix: Int, limit: U64, &stop: Size, &value: U64) -> Maybe(ReadError) =
    readDigitsAt(text, from, radix, limit, stop, value)

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The digit run at each magnitude width - one class, three instances, and the two targets split
   inside a function rather than inside an instance.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   `Magnitude(a -> w)` gives every integer a magnitude type and there are exactly three of them:
   `U32`, `U64` and the word's `USize`. This is the digit loop selected on that type, which is what
   lets one generic reader below serve twelve `Read` instances *and* `Format/Number.yana`'s styled
   reader without any of them naming a width.

   **A class and not a `@platform` pair of generic functions**, because the choice is not the same
   choice on the two targets. Natively the vectorized decimal reader is worth having at every width
   and the only question is a conversion; on JS the question is which widths get to stay a `number`,
   and it is the only question that matters. A class keyed on `w` states both at once: each instance
   is one line, and the instruction selection is the instance rather than a test in a loop.

   The `@platform` split is inside the three functions the instances call, so an instance is one body
   on both targets - which is where every other split in this library sits (Analysis-Modules.md §2.5)
   and keeps the class's shape out of the platform question.
-}
pub class DigitRun(w):
  fn readMagnitudeAt(text: String, from: Size, radix: Int, limit: w, &stop: Size, &value: w) -> Maybe(ReadError)

{-
   Natively, every width reads through the `U64` loop above and narrows on the way out.

   A `U64` is a register on this target, so the vector reader is free to every caller and the two
   conversions are nothing: `widen` on the limit is a zero extension the optimizer folds against a
   constant bound, and the `truncate` is exact because `readMagnitude` refuses anything above the
   limit it was handed. What it buys is that the 3.45x and 1.45x measured in §62.4 are not a `U64`'s
   privilege - `readSize` and `read(...) :: Int` get them too, where a width-specialized scalar loop
   would have given each of them its own slower copy.

   The rung is `Widen` up and `Narrow` down rather than a `bitcast` pair, because `USize` is not
   sixty-four bits on every target this body could be compiled for and a `bitcast` would be a
   silent lie on the one where it is not.
-}
@platform(native) fn (Ord(w), TrivialCopy(w), Widen(w, U64), Narrow(U64, w))
  readMagnitudeWide(text: String, from: Size, radix: Int, limit: w, &stop: Size, &value: w) -> Maybe(ReadError):
    let &wide = 0 :: U64

    if readMagnitude(text, from, radix, widen(limit) :: U64, stop, wide) is Just(failure):
        return Just(failure)

    value = truncate(wide) :: w
    return Nothing

-- And on JS the scalar loop at the caller's own width, which is the whole point of the width: a
-- `U32` and a `USize` are a `number` there and a `U64` is a `BigInt`, so a reader that widened first
-- would pay a bignum per digit to read a value that is not one.
@platform(native) fn readMagnitude32(text: String, from: Size, radix: Int, limit: U32, &stop: Size, &value: U32) -> Maybe(ReadError) =
    readMagnitudeWide(text, from, radix, limit, stop, value)

@platform(js) fn readMagnitude32(text: String, from: Size, radix: Int, limit: U32, &stop: Size, &value: U32) -> Maybe(ReadError) =
    readDigitsAt(text, from, radix, limit, stop, value)

@platform(native) fn readMagnitudeWord(text: String, from: Size, radix: Int, limit: USize, &stop: Size, &value: USize) -> Maybe(ReadError) =
    readMagnitudeWide(text, from, radix, limit, stop, value)

@platform(js) fn readMagnitudeWord(text: String, from: Size, radix: Int, limit: USize, &stop: Size, &value: USize) -> Maybe(ReadError) =
    readDigitsAt(text, from, radix, limit, stop, value)

instance DigitRun(U32):
    fn readMagnitudeAt(text: String, from: Size, radix: Int, limit: U32, &stop: Size, &value: U32) -> Maybe(ReadError) =
        readMagnitude32(text, from, radix, limit, stop, value)

instance DigitRun(USize):
    fn readMagnitudeAt(text: String, from: Size, radix: Int, limit: USize, &stop: Size, &value: USize) -> Maybe(ReadError) =
        readMagnitudeWord(text, from, radix, limit, stop, value)

-- The widest one is the loop itself on both targets - natively the vector reader, on JS the scalar
-- one at `U64`. This is the one instance a `BigInt` is the honest answer for, and a program reaches
-- it only by naming a 64-bit type.
instance DigitRun(U64):
    fn readMagnitudeAt(text: String, from: Size, radix: Int, limit: U64, &stop: Size, &value: U64) -> Maybe(ReadError) =
        readMagnitude(text, from, radix, limit, stop, value)

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   An integer of any type, in any radix - the one body every `Read` instance below is a call to.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   This replaced `readBounded` and `readUnsigned`, which were the same function written twice: one
   took a `Long` pair of bounds and applied a sign, the other took a `U64` limit and refused one, and
   both accumulated at sixty-four bits whatever they were reading into. `Magnitude(a -> w)` answers
   all three questions - what the bounds are, what the accumulator's width is, and whether the type
   has a sign at all - so the two collapse into one and the width comes out of the type instead of
   out of the widest one there is.

   **Whether a sign is read at all is `magnitude(minValue())`**, and it folds to a constant at every
   instantiation: a signed type's most negative magnitude is `2^(n-1)` and an unsigned type's is
   zero. So `-1` at a `U32` is still `NoDigits` - "this is not a number, try something else" - rather
   than a number this type refuses, which is the behaviour `readUnsigned` had and the one a parser
   that backtracks wants. `Format/Number.yana`'s styled reader deliberately differs: it reads the
   sign and lets `fromMagnitude` refuse it, because a *style* says a sign may be written and the type
   is what says it may not be held.

   The limit handed to the digit loop is the sign's own, so a magnitude that cannot fit stops the
   loop rather than wrapping it, and `fromMagnitude` re-checks what it already knows - one folded
   comparison, and the member that turns a magnitude and a sign back into a value.
-}
pub fn (Magnitude(a, w), Bounded(a), DigitRun(w), Integral(w), Ord(w), TrivialCopy(w))
  readInteger(from: String, &at: Size, radix: Int) -> Result(ReadError, a):
    let lowest = magnitude(minValue() :: a)

    let &start = at
    let &negative = False

    if lowest != 0 && start < length(from):
        let unit = String.unit(from, start)
        if unit == "-":
            negative = True
            start = start + 1
        else if unit == "+":
            start = start + 1

    -- The largest magnitude this sign admits, which is one larger on the negative side.
    let limit = if negative then lowest else magnitude(maxValue() :: a)

    let &stop = start
    let &size = 0 :: w

    if readMagnitudeAt(from, start, radix, limit, stop, size) is Just(failure):
        return Err(failure)

    let Just(->value) = fromMagnitude(size, negative) :: Maybe(a) | return Err(OutOfRange)

    at = stop
    return Ok(value)

{-
   The radix form, which is the whole family `readHex`, `readOctal` and `readBinary` would have been.

   Answers a `Long` because that is the widest thing every integer instance below reads through;
   a caller who wants a narrower type asks `read` for it and gets the range check with it.
-}
pub fn readRadix(from: String, &at: Size, radix: Int) -> Result(ReadError, Long) =
    readInteger(from, at, radix)

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The instances - twelve types, one line each, and no width written down in any of them.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Each was a `readBounded` or a `readUnsigned` with its own bounds widened into a `Long` and its own
   result truncated back out - four lines apiece, and the same accumulator behind all twelve. The
   bounds and the accumulator both come out of `Magnitude` now, so what is left is which type is
   being read into, and the expected type already says that.

   `Read(Size)` and `Read(USize)` are not a special case and never were: `Bounded` answers at the
   target's own width, so `read` at a `Size` rejects `3000000000` where the words are thirty-two bits
   and accepts it where they are sixty-four. That is the same answer `let n: Size = 3000000000`
   gives, which is the property worth having - a text that parses is a text the type could have held.
   What is new is that the magnitude behind it is the *word's* unsigned type rather than a `U64`, so
   `readSize` on JS is a `number` throughout.
-}
instance Read(I8):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, I8) = readInteger(from, at, 10)

instance Read(I16):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, I16) = readInteger(from, at, 10)

instance Read(I32):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, I32) = readInteger(from, at, 10)

instance Read(I64):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, I64) = readInteger(from, at, 10)

instance Read(U8):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, U8) = readInteger(from, at, 10)

instance Read(U16):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, U16) = readInteger(from, at, 10)

instance Read(U32):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, U32) = readInteger(from, at, 10)

instance Read(U64):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, U64) = readInteger(from, at, 10)

instance Read(Size):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Size) = readInteger(from, at, 10)

instance Read(USize):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, USize) = readInteger(from, at, 10)

instance Read(CodeUnit):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, CodeUnit) = readInteger(from, at, 10)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The typed readers - `read` and `readWith` with the answer's type in the name.
   ==========================================================================================

   `read` and `readWith` are selected by the expected type, which is the right rule and is a poor
   call site when nothing else in the expression says what the type is. `match read(text, at):`
   has no expected type at all, so it has to be written

       match read(text, at) :: Result(ReadError, Int):

   and the ascription is longer than the call. These are that call with the type said once, in the
   name, where a reader of the line wants it anyway: `readInt(text, at)`.

   **They are not a second mechanism.** Each is one line over the class, so an instance is still the
   only thing to write for a new type and `readWith` is still what a style is handed to. What they
   remove is a coercion, not a choice - `Core/Format/Number.yana`'s `readHexInt` and the rest are the
   same one line over `readWith`.

   The set is the widths a program reads by name. Anything else - a `U16` port, a `CodeUnit` - goes
   through `read` with the ascription it was going to write in the binding anyway.
-}
pub fn readInt(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Int) = read(from, at)
pub fn readLong(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Long) = read(from, at)
pub fn readUInt(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, UInt) = read(from, at)
pub fn readULong(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, ULong) = read(from, at)
pub fn readSize(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Size) = read(from, at)
